# MODEL COMPARISON — OLS vs TREE MODELS ON THE CHRONOLOGICAL HOLD-OUT
################################################################################
N = 397 effective days | chronological split: train n=318  hold-out n=79
Features (16): ['directions', 'directions_lag1', 'directions_lag2', 'directions_lag3', 'directions_roll7', 'precip', 'temp', 'sun', 'wind', 'precip_lag1', 'is_weekend_or_holiday', 'weather_severity', 'dow_mean_count', 'weekend_x_severity', 'weekend_x_intent', 'month']

[OLS]                hold-out R² = 0.6565  MAE = 1802.4
[RF baseline]        hold-out R² = 0.5120  MAE = 1893.2
[RF best-of-search]  hold-out R² = 0.5182  MAE = 1857.5  (max_depth=10, min_samples_leaf=3, max_features=0.7)
[XGBoost]            hold-out R² = 0.5695  MAE = 1796.6
[LightGBM]           hold-out R² = 0.5473  MAE = 1952.3

==============================================================================
RESULTS — chronological hold-out (318 train / 79 hold-out), sorted by R²
==============================================================================
Family    Model                                      Hold-out R²       MAE
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
OLS       OLS (linear, reference)                         0.6565    1802.4
XGBoost   XGBoost (gradient-boosted trees)                0.5695    1796.6
LightGBM  LightGBM (gradient-boosted trees)               0.5473    1952.3
RF        RF best config (from 120-config search)         0.5182    1857.5
RF        RF baseline (paper config)                      0.5120    1893.2
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
OLS hold-out R²         = 0.6565
Best tree-based model   = 0.5695
=> OLS OUTPERFORMS every tree-based model on the chronological hold-out.

--- Thesis-quoted reference values (Ch. 5) ---
  OLS hold-out R²             = 0.657
  baseline RF hold-out R²     = 0.512
  best RF (120-config search) = 0.522
  XGBoost best hold-out R²    = 0.597
  LightGBM hold-out R²        = 0.589
  Conclusion: OLS outperforms all 27 evaluated model variants.

Note: the full 120-configuration randomized RF search reported in the
thesis is cited above; this script evaluates its winning configuration
directly (deterministic) alongside XGBoost and LightGBM. Boosting models
beat the baseline RF but none reach OLS on the chronological hold-out.
